| `agents.tool_gate_margin_seconds` | integer | `150` | a whole number, 1 or more and at most `threading.TIMEOUT_MAX` less `make_targets.gate_timeout_seconds` and twice `make_targets.gate_stop_grace_seconds`, all of the same tree | `scripts/resource_limits.py` (`tool_limits`), for the same five callers as `agents.tool_timeout_seconds` | Sets the seconds `agents.tool_timeout_seconds` must leave over `make_targets.gate_timeout_seconds` plus twice `make_targets.gate_stop_grace_seconds`, for what the tool child does around the gate: its own start; in the gated apply child, `scripts/apply_refactor.py` run by an agent's `_run`, `plan_edits` and the writes before the gate and the rollback after a red one, including journal, target and recovery fsyncs; and the gate runner's start and its two tree snapshots; a larger margin refuses a shorter tool limit, with exit 14 before the child starts. A margin past its most leaves no `agents.tool_timeout_seconds` within that key's own ceiling to accept, so `tool_limits` refuses it with exit 14, the line naming this key and the most it can be. The start and both snapshots took about 0.3 s on a small tree. Two snapshots of a 200,000-file tree on local disk took about 7 s. Both snapshots took up to 65 s at 50,000 listed paths on an NFS home. The Default is twice 65 s plus 20 s, so it covers the NFS case even if 65 s was one snapshot. Absent, the reader writes `[using default] agents.tool_gate_margin_seconds = 150` to stderr. | the gate's own limit: `make_targets.gate_timeout_seconds`; its stop grace: `make_targets.gate_stop_grace_seconds` | always |
